{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>רַק אִם שָׁמוֹעַ, etc.</b> Scripture says this statement adjacent to the statement ״לֹא יִהְיֶה בְּךָ אֶבְיוֹן״, because wealth and poverty, according to the words of the Sages, whose memory is for a blessing (Mo'ed Katan 28a), are not dependent on merit. Therefore it says, adjacent to the promise of preventing poverty, <b>רַק אִם שָׁמוֹעַ</b>, etc. — meaning, if Israel keeps everything stated in the verse: <b>שָׁמוֹעַ תִּשְׁמַע</b> — this is the study of Torah, to learn and to teach; <b>לִשְׁמֹר</b> — these are negative commandments; and <b>לַעֲשׂוֹת</b> — these are positive commandments. And Scripture concludes by saying <b>אֶת כָּל</b>, etc. — meaning, that there should not be in his hand the neglect of any negative commandment nor the failure of any positive commandment. Only then is he included in the promise that there will be no poor person among him. For the statement of our Sages, whose memory is for a blessing, that it is not dependent on merit, means that even if his merits are abundant, as long as he has even one sin, he cannot master the mazal that shows poverty upon him. But when he reaches perfection in Torah and commandments, he masters even the mazal and attains wealth, children, and life."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:17:32.349102283Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}